(Regression test: a recent fix correctly stopped flagging an EXACT fit
of a fixed buffer as an error, but as a side effect also stopped
flagging GENUINE overflow (more data waiting than fits) - meaning data
was silently truncated with zero error signal. This checks the real
overflow case is still caught, and that no data is lost for a
subsequent read on the same fd (the fix peeks one byte ahead and
seeks back on overflow).)

open a file for writing called out at "./tmp_overflow_regression.txt".
Write "0123456789ABCDEF" to out.
Close out.

open a file for reading called inp at "./tmp_overflow_regression.txt".
a buffer called "b1" is 5 bytes in size.
Read from inp into b1.
On error Print "overflow detected".
Print b1.

(No data should be lost - the next read must pick up exactly where
the first one left off, not skip the probed-and-seeked-back byte)
a buffer called "b2" is 20 bytes in size.
Read from inp into b2.
On error Print "BUG: second read should not error".
Print b2.
Close inp.

Delete the file "./tmp_overflow_regression.txt".
